# Syriac Content Guide

## Fonts and Typography
- The website uses a custom Syriac font called "Karshon" located in the fonts directory
- Syriac text is displayed right-to-left (RTL) with the direction: rtl CSS property
- Syriac text typically uses a larger font size (1.5-1.8em) for better readability

## Parallel Text Structure
Syriac content is typically displayed in a parallel structure with:
- Syriac text (right side or top on mobile)
- Malayalam translation (left side or bottom on mobile)

## HTML Structure for Parallel Text
```html
<div class="parallel-text">
    <div class="syriac-text">
        <!-- Syriac text here (RTL) -->
    </div>
    <div class="malayalam-text">
        <!-- Malayalam translation here -->
    </div>
</div>
```

## Key CSS Classes
- `.syriac-text` - For Syriac content with RTL direction
- `.syriac-title` - For Syriac headings
- `.malayalam-text` - For Malayalam translations
- `.parallel-text` - Container for side-by-side display

## Responsive Design Notes
On mobile screens (max-width: 768px), the parallel text structure changes from horizontal to vertical layout, with Syriac text on top and Malayalam text below.
